{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["The sense of <b>וְעַל חַרְבְּךָ תִחְיֶה</b> (\"by your sword you shall live\") is like בְּחַרְבְּךָ (\"through your sword\"), and likewise \"for not by bread alone does man live\" (Deuteronomy 8:3) means by bread. The blessing is not that his sustenance will come from the plunder of enemies by his sword, for behold He gave him the fatness of the earth and the dew of the heavens, and by them he shall live. Rather, the meaning is to say that he will live through his wars, for he will prevail and not fall by the sword of an enemy. And therefore He said, \"and your brother you shall serve\" — that you will not prevail over him, and he will prevail over you." "<b>וְהָיָה כַּאֲשֶׁר תָּרִיד</b> (\"and it shall come to pass when you grow restive\") — according to your opinion, this hints at his breaking free from his hand: \"and you shall break his yoke from off your neck.\" It alludes to Israel that they should not provoke them excessively, to do harm to them. And this is what Scripture commanded (Deuteronomy 2:4–5), \"Take good heed to yourselves; do not contend with them,\" and so forth. Thus our Rabbis recalled (Jerusalem Talmud, Rosh HaShanah 1:1): they said, concerning \"For six months Joab and all Israel remained there, until he had cut off every male in Edom\" (I Kings 11:16) — the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: I said, \"Do not contend with them,\" and you contended with them. By your life, these six months are not counted to you and to your kingdom. For it is written (ibid. 2:11), \"And the days that David reigned over Israel were forty years,\" yet he reigned six months more, as it is written (ibid. 5:5), \"In Hebron he reigned over Judah seven years and six months.\" And further they said (Genesis Rabbah 1:16), the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: David, your hands are sharpened and zealous, and I seek to govern My world with them, and so forth."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes 